            <ArticleTitle>Elevated corticosterone in the amygdala leads to persistent increases in anxiety-like behavior and pain sensitivity.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>465-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.bbr.2010.05.049</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Corticosterone (CORT) localized to the amygdala induces anxiety-like behavior coupled with increased behavioral responses to visceral and somatic stimuli. In the current study, we investigated the long-term consequences of briefly exposing the amygdala to elevated levels of CORT with the hypothesis that modulation of the amygdala with CORT results in persistent increases in anxiety-like behavior and viscerosomatic sensitivity.</AbstractText>
